                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Incredible Dracula is the first time management game I've been compelled to rate poorly.  Considering the high production quality, you'd think that's a pretty tough thing to pull off; but when it comes to the genre, it's ALL about the gameplay.  The best titles are those that reward strategy and smart play, the worst will have you pulling your hair out from unending frustration.  Unfortunately, Incredible Dracula: Chasing Love is most definitely the latter.\n\nThere are a few things that a good time management game needs: responsive controls, the ability to chain tasks, and interesting level designs that allow for creative solutions.  Then there's the tuning which should be tight, but not so tight that absolute perfection is needed to achieve your goal.  This is where Dracula fails.  Early on, getting 3 stars on Multit-Click Mode (which is basically Expert in every other game) becomes rage inducing as the levels are not only long, but leave no room for error.  It may not be an issue on Casual Mode in which you have unlimited time, but I play these games to challenge myself.  Learning the levels, devising a strategy and nailing the execution is what makes time management games so enjoyable.  But when I'm following the in-game strategy guide to the T and still come up sorely short on time, there's something very, very wrong.\n\nTo make matters worse, restarting the level (which you'll end up doing several times) is bogged down with a forced tutorial that you have to click through every time it's replayed.  You can opt to skip it within the level itself, but then you're required to click through a large confirmation box for it. While this may sound like such a little thing, when you're focusing on figuring out a level, having to jump through hoops every time you restart is disruptive to the thought process.  The ability to turn tutorials completely off in the options would have greatly improved the experience and eased some of my frustrations, but it's conspicuously absent.\n\nThe story itself is unique but may be off-putting to some (Dracula is on the run from his overweight, ugly and lovesick admirer).  The graphics are pleasant enough, and the monster theme has some cute ideas, but nothing ultimately memorable.  The best part of the game is the soundtrack which features lovely classically-styled orchestral themes.  I could listen to it for hours, but forcing myself through more of the gameplay would have me suicidal by the end. \n\nI really wanted to love Incredible Dracula: Chasing Love.  Alawar is one of my favorite casual game developers and they consistently put out great products, but I simply could not get past the insane difficulty and logistical annoyances.  While plenty of games in the genre are generic and uninspired, at least they provide enjoyable gameplay - that's not the case here.  I'll likely swap over to Casual Mode to 100% the achievements so I can move on.  No challenge, no strategy, just going through the motions to finish what I started.  Perhaps some of the problems were fixed in the sequel, but I'm not sure I'm willing to give it a chance after this disappointment.\n\n[code][h1]Curated by: [url=https://store.steampowered.com/curator/33004802-Chudah%27s-Corner/]Chudah's Corner[/url][/h1][/code]",
